/** \addtogroup area_intractable */
/** \defgroup theory_intractable Theory of intractable problems
 \ingroup area_intractable

 \details The headers of `include/ADS/intractable` realize chapter 19 of the
 book, i.e., the ways of facing a problem that is NP-hard (see the theory of
 complexity, `include/ADS/complexity`): the pseudopolynomial algorithms, the
 approximation algorithms, the branch-&-bound, the heuristics, and the search
 of the fifteen puzzle. This page collects what the headers take for
 granted: the notation of the problems of optimization (see
 \ref theory_intractable_notation) and, for each strategy, the property on
 which its guarantee rests and the argument behind its bound (from
 \ref theory_intractable_pseudo to \ref theory_intractable_puzzle). The
 pseudocode of each algorithm is instead on the page of its header, in the
 comment of its function, together with the section of the book, or the
 paper, that it follows.

 \tableofcontents

 \section theory_intractable_notation Definitions and notation

 A problem of optimization asks, for an instance \f$ x \f$, a feasible
 solution of least cost (or of largest value) among the set \f$ F( x ) \f$
 of the feasible ones; \f$ x^* \f$ is an optimal solution and
 \f$ \mathit{OPT} = c( x^* ) \f$ its cost. When the decision version is
 NP-complete, no algorithm is known that finds \f$ x^* \f$ in polynomial
 time on every instance, and the strategies of the area give up one of the
 three: the polynomial time in the size (the pseudopolynomial algorithms and
 the branch-&-bound), the optimum (the approximations and the heuristics),
 or every instance (the special cases of the theory of complexity).

 <table>
 <caption>The symbols of the headers of the area</caption>
 <tr><th>Symbol</th><th>Meaning</th></tr>
 <tr><td>\f$ c( x ) \f$, \f$ x^* \f$, \f$ \mathit{OPT} \f$</td>
     <td>the cost of a feasible solution, an optimal solution and its
         cost</td></tr>
 <tr><td>\f$ d \f$, \f$ \# \f$</td>
     <td>the size of the instance and its largest integer</td></tr>
 <tr><td>\f$ e \f$, \f$ \epsilon \f$</td>
     <td>the relative error of an approximation, a constant of the
         algorithm or an input of it</td></tr>
 <tr><td>\f$ \mathit{lb}( S ) \f$, \f$ \mathit{minCost} \f$</td>
     <td>a lower bound of the costs of the solutions that extend the
         partial solution \f$ S \f$, and the cost of the best solution found
         so far</td></tr>
 <tr><td>\f$ I( \pi ) \f$, \f$ I_k( \pi ) \f$</td>
     <td>a neighborhood of the solution \f$ \pi \f$, and the circuits that
         share \f$ n - k \f$ edges with the circuit \f$ \pi \f$</td></tr>
 <tr><td>\f$ D \f$, \f$ p_i \f$, \f$ v_i \f$, \f$ C \f$</td>
     <td>the distances of the salesman, and the profits, the volumes and the
         capacity of the knapsack</td></tr>
 </table>

 \section theory_intractable_pseudo Pseudopolynomial algorithms

 An algorithm is <em>pseudopolynomial</em> if its time is bounded by a
 polynomial in \f$ d \f$ and \f$ \# \f$ (section 19.1); since \f$ \# \f$ may
 be exponential in \f$ d \f$, such an algorithm is polynomial only on the
 instances whose numbers are small. For SUBSET SUM, with \f$ M[ i ][ j ] \f$
 true if some of the first \f$ i \f$ elements sum to \f$ j \f$,
 \f[
   M[ i ][ j ] = M[ i - 1 ][ j ] \ \text{or}
   \ ( j \ge A_i \ \text{and} \ M[ i - 1 ][ j - A_i ] ) , \tag{1}
 \f]
 since a subset of the first \f$ i \f$ elements either avoids the
 \f$ i \f$-th or takes it once; `subsetSumDP()` of `Pseudopolynomial.h` fills
 (1) in \f$ O( n k ) \f$ time, with one row of \f$ k + 1 \f$ bits written
 from the right, so that \f$ M[ j - A_i ] \f$ is still the value of the row
 before, and `subsetSumSet()` keeps all the rows to read a subset backwards.
 The same table with three parts gives `bottino()`, whose
 \f$ S[ i ][ j ][ h ] \f$ has \f$ O( n m^2 ) \f$ entries, and the vectors of
 the loads give `schedulingDP()`, with at most \f$ ( d + 1 )^m \f$ vectors
 after each program.

 A problem is <em>strongly NP-complete</em> if it stays NP-complete on the
 instances with \f$ \# \f$ bounded by a polynomial in \f$ d \f$ (CLIQUE, whose
 numbers are bounded by \f$ n \f$, TRAVELING SALESMAN, 3-PARTITION), and
 <em>weakly</em> otherwise (SUBSET SUM, PARTITION, KNAPSACK).

 <em>Theorem 1.</em> A strongly NP-complete problem has no pseudopolynomial
 algorithm unless \f$ \mathrm{P} = \mathrm{NP} \f$ (Theorem 19.1 of the
 book).

 Indeed, on the instances with \f$ \# \le q( d ) \f$, \f$ q \f$ a polynomial,
 the time of a pseudopolynomial algorithm is a polynomial in \f$ d \f$ and
 \f$ q( d ) \f$, i.e., in \f$ d \f$; such an algorithm would solve in
 polynomial time a restriction that is NP-complete.

 When a program may be interrupted and resumed on another processor, the
 scheduling becomes easy: no schedule is shorter than
 \f[
   d = \max\Big\{ \max_i t_i , \ \sum_i t_i / m \Big\} , \tag{2}
 \f]
 since each program runs for its time and the \f$ m \f$ processors do at most
 \f$ m d \f$ work, and the rule of McNaughton (`mcNaughton()`) reaches (2) by
 filling the processors one after the other up to \f$ d \f$. A program split
 between the processors \f$ q \f$ and \f$ q + 1 \f$ runs on \f$ q \f$ at the
 end and on \f$ q + 1 \f$ at the start, and the two pieces do not overlap in
 time because \f$ t_i \le d \f$. With \f$ t_i = C_i / T_i \f$ and
 \f$ d = 1 \f$, the same rule schedules a period of the periodic tasks of
 `realTimeScheduling()`, when \f$ \sum_i C_i / T_i \le m \f$.

 \section theory_intractable_approx Approximation algorithms

 An algorithm is an <em>approximation</em> with relative error \f$ e \f$ if
 it gives a feasible \f$ x' \f$ with
 \f[
   \frac{| c( x' ) - \mathit{OPT} |}{\mathit{OPT}} \le e \tag{3}
 \f]
 on every instance, and an <em>approximation scheme</em> if it takes
 \f$ \epsilon > 0 \f$ in input and satisfies (3) with \f$ e = \epsilon \f$,
 in a time polynomial in \f$ d \f$ for each fixed \f$ \epsilon \f$ (fully
 polynomial if also in \f$ 1 / \epsilon \f$). The guarantees of the area rest
 on a lower bound of \f$ \mathit{OPT} \f$ that the algorithm can compare with
 its own cost.

 <table>
 <caption>The approximations of the area (section 19.2 of the book and the
          exercises 19.6 and 19.8)</caption>
 <tr><th>Problem</th><th>Function</th><th>Guarantee</th><th>Time</th></tr>
 <tr><td>BIN PACKING</td><td><tt>firstFit()</tt></td>
     <td>\f$ N < 2 N^* \f$</td><td>\f$ O( n^2 ) \f$</td></tr>
 <tr><td>TRAVELING SALESMAN, triangular</td><td><tt>deltaTsp()</tt></td>
     <td>\f$ c( x' ) \le 2\, \mathit{OPT} \f$</td>
     <td>\f$ O( n^2 \log n ) \f$</td></tr>
 <tr><td>SUBSET SUM</td><td><tt>apSubsetSum()</tt></td>
     <td>\f$ e < 1 / ( h + 1 ) \f$</td><td>\f$ O( h n^{h+1} ) \f$</td></tr>
 <tr><td>VERTEX COVER</td><td><tt>vertexCoverApprox()</tt></td>
     <td>\f$ |S| \le 2\, \mathit{OPT} \f$</td><td>\f$ O( n^2 ) \f$</td></tr>
 <tr><td>KNAPSACK</td><td><tt>knapsackApprox()</tt></td>
     <td>\f$ c( x' ) \ge ( 1 - \epsilon ) \mathit{OPT} \f$</td>
     <td>\f$ O( n^3 / \epsilon ) \f$</td></tr>
 </table>

 For BIN PACKING, the first fit never leaves two boxes whose contents sum to
 at most \f$ k \f$, since the objects of the later one would have fitted in
 the earlier one. Summing the contents of the \f$ N \ge 2 \f$ pairs of
 consecutive boxes (the last with the first) counts each box twice, so that
 twice the total volume \f$ V \f$ exceeds \f$ N k \f$; since
 \f$ N^* \ge V / k \f$, one has \f$ N < 2 V / k \le 2 N^* \f$, and the
 decreasing order of ffd improves the constant (to \f$ 11 / 9 \f$
 asymptotically, by Johnson et al.). For the triangular salesman, a minimum
 spanning tree \f$ T \f$ weighs less than any circuit, which is a spanning
 path plus an edge, and the walk that goes twice along each edge of
 \f$ T \f$ costs \f$ 2 w( T ) \f$; `deltaTsp()` takes the cities in the order
 of their first visit, i.e., in preorder, and each shortcut over the cities
 already visited costs no more than the stretch it replaces, by the triangle
 inequality. Without the triangle inequality there is no such constant.

 <em>Theorem 2.</em> For no constant \f$ s \ge 1 \f$ does TRAVELING SALESMAN
 have a polynomial algorithm with \f$ c( x' ) \le s\, \mathit{OPT} \f$,
 unless \f$ \mathrm{P} = \mathrm{NP} \f$ (Theorem 19.2 of the book).

 Indeed, with the distances of `tspGap()`, 1 on the edges of \f$ G \f$ and
 \f$ s n \f$ elsewhere, a Hamiltonian circuit of \f$ G \f$ is a tour of cost
 \f$ n \f$, while any other tour costs more than \f$ s n \f$; an algorithm
 within \f$ s \f$ would therefore return a tour of cost at most \f$ s n \f$
 exactly when \f$ G \f$ has a Hamiltonian circuit, and decide HAMILTONIAN
 CIRCUIT in polynomial time.

 For SUBSET SUM, let \f$ S^* \f$ be optimal, of sum \f$ z^* \le k \f$. If
 \f$ |S^*| \le h \f$, `apSubsetSum()` tries \f$ S^* \f$ itself; otherwise it
 tries the \f$ h \f$ largest elements of \f$ S^* \f$, and the greedy
 completion on the elements in decreasing order either takes all of
 \f$ S^* \f$, or leaves out an element \f$ a \f$ of \f$ S^* \f$, not larger
 than those \f$ h \f$, with \f$ z + a > k \ge z^* \f$. Since
 \f$ ( h + 1 ) a \le z^* \f$, the sum found has
 \f[
   z > z^* - a \ge z^* \Big( 1 - \frac{1}{h + 1} \Big) , \tag{4}
 \f]
 and the \f$ O( n^h ) \f$ sets of at most \f$ h \f$ elements, each completed
 in \f$ O( n ) \f$, give the time. For VERTEX COVER the edges taken by
 `vertexCoverApprox()` share no end, i.e., form a matching \f$ M \f$; a cover
 has an end of each edge of \f$ M \f$, hence \f$ \mathit{OPT} \ge |M| \f$,
 while the cover returned has \f$ 2 |M| \f$ nodes. For KNAPSACK, the profits
 rounded down to multiples of \f$ K = \epsilon p_{\max} / n \f$ lose less
 than \f$ K \f$ per object, i.e., less than \f$ n K = \epsilon p_{\max} \f$
 on any set; the set optimal for the rounded profits is therefore within
 \f$ \epsilon p_{\max} \le \epsilon\, \mathit{OPT} \f$ of the optimum, since
 each object fits alone. The table of `knapsackApprox()` has a column per
 rounded profit, at most \f$ n \cdot n / \epsilon \f$ of them, and a row per
 object, whence \f$ O( n^3 / \epsilon ) \f$, a fully polynomial scheme (that
 of Ibarra and Kim).

 \section theory_intractable_bb Branch-&-bound

 A branch-&-bound visits the tree of the partial solutions of the
 enumeration of chapter 16 and skips a subtree when a lower bound of the
 costs in it is not below the best cost found (section 19.3).

 <em>Lemma 3 (the bound).</em> If \f$ \mathit{lb}( S ) \le c( x ) \f$ for each
 complete solution \f$ x \f$ that extends \f$ S \f$, then skipping \f$ S \f$
 when
 \f[
   \mathit{lb}( S ) \ge \mathit{minCost} \tag{5}
 \f]
 loses no solution better than the best found, and the visit returns an
 optimum.

 The guarantee holds whatever the order of visit and the bound, and the time
 in the worst case stays that of the enumeration; what changes is the number
 of nodes in practice, which `bbTsp()`, `littleTsp()`, `maxWeightClique()`
 and the knapsacks report in `*generated`. The bounds of the area satisfy
 the hypothesis of Lemma 3 as follows. In the bound of the text
 (`TspBound::BOOK` of `tspLowerBound()`), a circuit that starts with the
 path \f$ S \f$ enters each city out of \f$ S \f$ once and leaves it once,
 and goes back to \f$ S[ 0 ] \f$ from a city out of \f$ S \f$; the least cost
 of entering and leaving each city, \f$ D_h \f$, counts each edge of the rest
 of the circuit at most twice, and half the sum, with \f$ A \f$ and
 \f$ B \f$, is a lower bound of that rest. In the bound of the 1-tree
 (`TspBound::ONE_TREE`), the rest of the circuit is a path through the cities
 out of \f$ S \f$ plus two edges, and a path is a spanning tree; the weight
 of a minimum spanning tree, plus the least edges \f$ A \f$ and \f$ B \f$,
 is therefore a lower bound (Held and Karp). In the reduction of
 `littleTsp()`, subtracting a constant from a row or a column of the matrix
 lowers the cost of every circuit by that constant, since a circuit uses
 exactly one entry of each row and of each column; the sum of the constants
 subtracted is a lower bound, and the entries 0 left are the edges that a
 circuit of that cost would use. `littleTsp()` visits by least bound, so that
 the first complete circuit extracted is optimal: any other node has a bound
 at least as large. `maxWeightClique()` bounds a set by its weight plus that
 of all its candidates, and `knapsackBB()` by the fractional knapsack of the
 objects left, which is at least the profit of any set of them within the
 capacity left, since the fractional greedy by \f$ p_i / v_i \f$ is optimal
 for the relaxation (chapter 14).

 \section theory_intractable_heur Heuristics

 A heuristic gives a feasible solution with no guarantee on its cost (section
 19.4); it is judged by its time and by its results in practice. The
 constructive heuristics of the area are greedy. `greedyTsp()` keeps the
 lightest edges that leave every city with at most two of them and close no
 cycle, as `kruskal()` does with an MFSET, and so ends with a Hamiltonian
 chain, closed by the edge of its two ends; the sort of the
 \f$ n ( n - 1 ) / 2 \f$ edges gives \f$ O( n^2 \log n ) \f$.
 `greedySuperstring()` is the same greedy on the oriented edges weighed by
 the overlaps, which leaves oriented paths. `minMaxGreedy()` grows the two
 halves of a bisection in turn, each time with the node out of them that
 has the fewest edges to the other half; the structure `Buckets` keeps the
 nodes by their count of edges to each half, with each node at a known place
 of its bucket and the least and largest bucket not empty, so that a node
 moves to the next bucket in \f$ O( 1 ) \f$, and the scans of the bounds,
 which only move forward between two moves back, cost \f$ O( 1 ) \f$
 amortized. `dsatur()` colors first the node that sees the most colors, which
 is the most constrained.

 A <em>local search</em> starts from a solution \f$ \pi \f$ and moves to a
 better solution of its neighborhood \f$ I( \pi ) \f$ while there is one; it
 ends at a <em>local optimum</em>, since the cost decreases strictly at each
 step and the solutions are finitely many. For the salesman, the
 neighborhood \f$ I_2( \pi ) \f$ of `twoOpt()` holds the circuits obtained
 by removing two edges of \f$ \pi \f$ that do not touch and reconnecting the
 two paths in the only other way, i.e., by reversing the stretch between
 them, which gives
 \f[
   | I_2( \pi ) | = \frac{n ( n - 1 )}{2} - n , \tag{6}
 \f]
 the pairs of edges less the \f$ n \f$ that touch; the change of cost of a
 move depends on the four edges alone, and a step costs \f$ O( n^2 ) \f$.
 `kOptNeighbours()` counts \f$ | I_k( \pi ) | \f$ by brute force, which
 checks (6) for \f$ k = 2 \f$. `partitionLocalSearch()` does the same for
 PARTITION, with the moves of one element and the swaps of two, each
 changing the difference of the sums by twice the elements moved.

 \section theory_intractable_puzzle The fifteen puzzle

 `solvePuzzle()` of `FifteenPuzzle.h` visits the configurations by least
 \f$ f( x ) = g( x ) + h( x ) \f$, \f$ g( x ) \f$ the moves from the start and
 \f$ h( x ) \f$ the sum of the distances of the tiles from their places
 (`manhattan()`); this is the branch-&-bound of section 19.5 with the visit
 by least bound, i.e., the algorithm A* of Hart, Nilsson and Raphael.

 <em>Lemma 4 (A* with a consistent bound).</em> If \f$ h \f$ is 0 at the goal
 and
 \f[
   h( x ) \le 1 + h( y ) \quad \text{for each move from } x \text{ to } y ,
   \tag{7}
 \f]
 then the first goal extracted from the queue is reached in the fewest
 moves.

 Indeed, a move changes the place of one tile by one square, and so changes
 \f$ h \f$ by at most 1, which is (7); then \f$ f \f$ never decreases along a
 path, and \f$ h \f$ never exceeds the moves left. When a goal \f$ x \f$ is
 extracted, every node of a shorter solution would have
 \f$ f \le g^* < g( x ) = f( x ) \f$ and would have been extracted before it,
 with its successors. The map of the least level of each configuration lets
 a configuration enter the queue again only with fewer moves, and an entry
 with more moves than the map is stale and skipped; hence each configuration
 is expanded once. `puzzleBFS()` is the case \f$ h = 0 \f$, which examines far
 more configurations, and `puzzleDFS()` the backtracking of chapter 16 with
 a bound on the moves, which finds a solution within the bound if there is
 one, not necessarily the shortest.

 Half of the configurations cannot reach the goal. A move of the hole along a
 row leaves the order of the tiles, read by rows, unchanged, and a move along
 a column moves one tile past \f$ n - 1 \f$ others, which changes the number
 of inversions by an amount of the parity of \f$ n - 1 \f$; with \f$ n \f$ odd
 the parity of the inversions is therefore invariant, and with \f$ n \f$ even
 that of the inversions plus the row of the hole is. `solvable()` compares
 that parity with the goal (Johnson and Story), which is also sufficient.

